interpolated. Thereafter there is a passage of play with White's moves
appearing in the right hand column and Black's in the left)} Ba8 27. Bc4 Bg7
28. f4 g5 29. Bxd5 Bxd5 {(This recapture is missing but at least the score
reverts to White on left and Black on right)} 30. Kg1 gxf4 31. Bxf4 Be4 32. Nb5
Rxb5 33. Qxb5 Bxb1 34. Rxb1 Bxe5 35. Bxe5 Qxe5 {("QxQ")} 36. Qd3 f4 37. Rd1 Qg7
38. Kh1 Qg4 39. Qf3 Qg5 {From now on the score makes little sense: "Q-R4"} (
39... Qh4 40. Rc1 h5) 40. Rc1 h5 41. Qd3 {("Q-Q5" obviously impossible)} Qg4
42. Rf1 Qf5 {(I don't really believe the reconstruction of the last few moves
- JS) "Draw agreed after 6 moves". Source: Western Daily Press, 11 April 1928.
No. of moves = 52 according to The Times, 11 April 1928} 1/2-1/2

